{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghzpTo11TDVjCqXSzPWh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For high-spin [Co(H₂O)₆]²⁺ I get **Dq ≈ 922 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 828 cm⁻¹ and C ≈ 4264 cm⁻¹**. The two quartet bands fix Dq and B exactly, and the doublet band then fixes C.\n\n**1. Quartet bands give Dq and B**\n\nThe ground state is ⁴T₁g(F). In the ⁴F term, ⁴T₁ sits at −6Dq, ⁴T₂ at +2Dq and ⁴A₂ at +12Dq. ⁴P sits at 15B. The two ⁴T₁ states mix through an off-diagonal element of 4Dq. Solving that mixing gives the ⁴T₁ energies:\n\nE(⁴T₁) = 7.5B − 3Dq ± ½S, where S = √(225B² + 180B·Dq + 100Dq²)\n\nFrom this the two band positions are:\n- ν(⁴T₁(2)) = S = 19 400 cm⁻¹\n- ν(⁴T₂) = 5Dq − 7.5B + ½S = 8 100 cm⁻¹\n\nThe second equation gives 5Dq − 7.5B = −1 600, so Dq = 1.5B − 320.\n\nPutting this into S² = 19 400² gives 720B² − 153 600B − 366 120 000 = 0. The positive root is:\n- B = 827.7 cm⁻¹\n- Dq = 921.5 cm⁻¹ (so 10Dq ≈ 9 215 cm⁻¹)\n\n**2. Doublet band gives C**\n\nI diagonalised the full d⁷ matrices with Dq = 921.5 and B = 827.7 cm⁻¹, using the tool at two values of C.\n- **Quartet check:** both runs give ⁴T₂ = 8 100 cm⁻¹ and ⁴T₁(2) = 19 400 cm⁻¹, as they should, since these do not depend on C.\n- **⁴A₂ position:** it comes out at 17 315 cm⁻¹.\n- **²T₁(2) position:** it is 19 609 cm⁻¹ at C = 3600 and 21 409 cm⁻¹ at C = 4200. It therefore rises by exactly 3 cm⁻¹ for every 1 cm⁻¹ increase in C, so it is linear in C here.\n\nReaching 21 600 cm⁻¹ needs C = 4200 + (21 600 − 21 408.6)/3 ≈ 4264 cm⁻¹. That puts C/B at about 5.15. This value was solved from the linear trend, not from a third run at C = 4264.\n\n**Result:** Dq ≈ 922 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 828 cm⁻¹ (about 74 % of the free-ion value, roughly 1120 cm⁻¹), C ≈ 4264 cm⁻¹.\n\n{\"Dq\": 921.5, \"B\": 827.7, \"C\": 4264, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Quartet bands fix Dq and B analytically; the 2T1(2) band, which rises 3 cm-1 per cm-1 of C, fixes C.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop